WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.408
Welcome to the Securing Your Seller Account series.

00:00:05.408 --> 00:00:10.140
It is important to protect yourself from

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:15.960
phishing attacks as fraudsters might attempt to steal your personal, financial, or other

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:16.851
sensitive information.

00:00:16.851 --> 00:00:22.200
In this video, you'll learn what phishing is, how to protect yourself

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:26.614
from phishing, and how to recover from a phishing attack.

00:00:26.614 --> 00:00:28.380
You might receive emails

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:29.324
from Amazon.

00:00:29.324 --> 00:00:35.460
However, sometimes you may receive emails that are not really from Amazon, even

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:38.411
if at first glance they appear to be.

00:00:38.411 --> 00:00:42.100
Instead, such emails are faked in an attempt to get

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:46.358
you to reveal sensitive account or banking information.

00:00:46.358 --> 00:00:49.020
These fake emails, or what's

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:55.157
called spoofed or phishing emails, look similar to legitimate emails from Amazon.

00:00:55.157 --> 00:00:56.180
Often these

00:00:56.180 --> 00:01:02.140
emails direct you to a false website that looks similar to an Amazon website, where you

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:08.420
might be asked to give account information such as your email address and password combination.

00:01:08.420 --> 00:01:14.445
Remember, Amazon never asks you to verify sensitive information via email.

00:01:14.445 --> 00:01:15.540
Only enter

00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:21.980
information when completing an order on our website, registering to sell on Amazon or

00:01:21.980 --> 00:01:24.915
updating account information in Seller Central.

00:01:24.915 --> 00:01:28.340
To protect yourself from responding to these

00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:31.625
emails, you can follow some simple rules.

00:01:31.625 --> 00:01:35.380
Number one, know what Amazon won't ask in

00:01:35.380 --> 00:01:35.911
email.

00:01:35.911 --> 00:01:43.340
Amazon will not ask you for your bank account information, credit card number, PIN,

00:01:43.340 --> 00:01:49.511
your Seller Central account password, or credit card security code via email.

00:01:49.511 --> 00:01:50.540
Instead, Amazon

00:01:50.540 --> 00:01:58.180
will always ask you to update credit card details or account information via Seller Central.

00:01:58.180 --> 00:02:03.837
Number two, review the email for grammatical or typographical errors.

00:02:03.837 --> 00:02:06.100
Watch for poor grammar

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:07.549
or typographical errors.

00:02:07.549 --> 00:02:12.380
Many phishing emails are translated from other languages or are

00:02:12.380 --> 00:02:14.669
sent without being proofread.

00:02:14.669 --> 00:02:18.103
Number three, check the return address.

00:02:18.103 --> 00:02:19.820
Genuine emails from

00:02:19.820 --> 00:02:20.310
Amazon.

00:02:20.310 --> 00:02:25.210
com always come from an address ending in @amazon.

00:02:25.210 --> 00:02:25.700
com.

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:27.660
Check the email's header

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:28.122
information.

00:02:28.122 --> 00:02:35.060
If the received from reply to or return path for the email does not come

00:02:35.060 --> 00:02:36.380
from @amazon.

00:02:36.380 --> 00:02:39.020
com, it is not from Amazon.

00:02:39.020 --> 00:02:42.980
The method you use to check the header information

00:02:42.980 --> 00:02:46.415
varies depending upon the email program you use.

00:02:46.415 --> 00:02:49.420
The following are some examples of fraudulent

00:02:49.420 --> 00:02:51.180
return addresses.

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:54.700
Seller-performance@payments-amazon.

00:02:54.700 --> 00:02:58.220
com, Amazon-security@hotmail.

00:02:58.220 --> 00:03:01.740
com, or Amazon-payments
@

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:02.343
msn.

00:03:02.343 --> 00:03:02.946
com.

00:03:02.946 --> 00:03:07.769
Number four, review the email for mismatched hyperlinks. ,

00:03:08.974 --> 00:03:10.180
some phishing

00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:15.180
emails include a hyperlink or a button that looks as though it will take you to your Amazon

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:15.531
account.

00:03:15.531 --> 00:03:20.800
However, if you hover your mouse over the link, you often can see the underlying

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:27.900
false website address either as a pop-up or as information in the browser status bar.

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:31.727
When in doubt, do not click on the links in the email.

00:03:31.727 --> 00:03:33.640
Instead, open a new window and

00:03:33.640 --> 00:03:35.920
go directly to Amazon.

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:39.340
com or the Seller Central website.

00:03:39.340 --> 00:03:42.126
Number five, check the website address.

00:03:42.126 --> 00:03:45.840
Some phishers set up spoofed websites that look

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:51.786
similar to the Amazon website and use the word Amazon somewhere in the URL.

00:03:51.786 --> 00:03:53.060
We will never

00:03:53.060 --> 00:03:57.721
use a combination such as security-amazon.

00:03:57.721 --> 00:03:59.719
com or amazon.

00:03:59.719 --> 00:04:00.385
com.

00:04:00.385 --> 00:04:03.048
biz in the URL.

00:04:03.048 --> 00:04:04.380
Genuine Amazon

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:08.280
website URLs always end with .

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:09.060
amazon.

00:04:09.060 --> 00:04:11.400
com or sellercentral.

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:12.180
amazon.

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:12.960
com.

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:15.300
Always check that

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:23.880
the URL starts with HTTPS and is preceded by a secure lock icon.

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:28.940
If you've entered your password on what you think might be a malicious website, then change

00:04:28.940 --> 00:04:30.738
your password on amazon.

00:04:30.738 --> 00:04:31.636
com immediately.

00:04:31.636 --> 00:04:34.782
To do that, go to the amazon.

00:04:34.782 --> 00:04:36.580
com sign-in page

00:04:36.580 --> 00:04:41.740
and click on "Forgot your password." If you've entered your credit card information

00:04:41.740 --> 00:04:48.300
on what you think might be a malicious website or replied to an email with that information,

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:50.974
immediately contact your credit card company.

00:04:50.974 --> 00:04:54.540
Note, if you receive a new replacement card,

00:04:54.540 --> 00:04:59.127
then don't forget to update your new card on amazon.

00:04:59.127 --> 00:04:59.585
com.

00:04:59.585 --> 00:05:01.420
Amazon takes phishing attempts

00:05:01.420 --> 00:05:02.582
very seriously.

00:05:02.582 --> 00:05:10.138
To report a suspicious email or learn more about phishing, go to amazon.

00:05:10.138 --> 00:05:11.300
com/security.
